# Deep beginner lessons, appended into each answer. Part 1: Q1-Q15.
# Plain prose: no double quotes, no $ signs, no trailing backticks inside strings.
$LEARN = @{}

$LEARN[1] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> A custom agent is a saved persona for Copilot kept in a <b>.agent.md</b> file: a name, a description, a <b>tools</b> allow-list and instructions. The tools list is a permission boundary, exactly like giving a coworker a badge that opens only some doors. <b>search</b> finds files and code ('where is login handled?'), <b>read</b> opens them, <b>edit</b> rewrites them, <b>execute</b> runs shell commands, <b>agent</b> lets it delegate to subagents, <b>web</b> browses the internet, and entries like <b>microsoftdocs/mcp/docs_search</b> are Model Context Protocol tools that query Microsoft Learn docs. A planning-only agent such as implementation-planner must look but never touch, so it gets only search and read. <b>Example:</b> you ask it to plan API rate limiting. It searches for the auth code, reads the controllers and the Learn docs on throttling, then writes a step-by-step plan. With edit or execute it could rewrite your controllers unasked; with web it could leak internal code into public search queries. That is why plan-only means search plus read, nothing more.</div>"
$LEARN[2] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot app-modernization agent upgrades legacy applications in three visible stages, and each stage writes its own file under <b>.github/upgrades/{scenario}</b>. <b>Stage 1 Assessment</b> scans structure, dependencies and code patterns and writes <b>assessment.md</b>: breaking changes, deprecated APIs, upgrade scope. You review it, confirm strategy decisions (saved to upgrade-options.md), then <b>Stage 2 Planning</b> writes <b>plan.md</b> (the strategy in prose) plus <b>tasks.md</b> (the ordered checklist). <b>Stage 3 Execution</b> works the checklist top to bottom; every task cycles start, execute, validate (build and test), complete, and the agent rewrites tasks.md with live status markers as it goes. <b>Example:</b> upgrading App1 from .NET 6 to .NET 10, tasks.md shows TASK-001 project files [x] done, TASK-002 EF6 to EF Core [.] in progress, TASK-003 full test suite [ ] pending. Opening tasks.md answers 'which step are we on and did tests pass' at a glance. assessment.md cannot answer that: it was frozen before execution began.</div>"
$LEARN[3] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Every GitHub Actions run receives a <b>GITHUB_TOKEN</b>: a short-lived credential proving 'I am this workflow'. A <b>permissions</b> block scopes what that token may touch, and scopes are fine-grained: <b>contents</b> covers files and commits, <b>pull-requests</b> covers opening, commenting and approving PRs, <b>issues</b> covers issues. Permissions can be set once at workflow top (the default for all jobs) and tightened or widened per job. <b>Least privilege</b> means default read-only and grant write only to the job that must write. <b>Example:</b> the agent-pr workflow has an analyze job (reads PR content, posts a comment) and an update_artifacts job (regenerates files, creates a PR). Top-level contents: read keeps analyze honest; contents: write on update_artifacts lets it commit; pull-requests: write on the create step lets it open the PR. If the default were write, any compromised step, or any agent prompt-injection hidden in PR text, could push code or approve merges. Read-by-default shrinks that blast radius to zero.</div>"
$LEARN[4] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> You cannot watch an agent the way you watch a colleague, so GitHub gives reviewers three lenses with different time horizons. <b>Artifacts</b> (actions/upload-artifact, with a retention-days setting) persist files after the run ends: durable evidence you can download next week. The <b>PR body and timeline</b> are the running narrative on the pull request itself: the agent edits the PR description with decisions and every event lands in the timeline. <b>View session</b> streams the live log while the agent works. <b>Example:</b> Monday morning you open an agent PR from Friday. The timeline tells you it tried three approaches and picked one; you download plan.md from artifacts to read the reasoning; and when you ask for a revision, View session lets you watch it work in real time. Assigning issues starts work, approving workflows gates it, mentioning @copilot steers it: none of those records what happened, which is why they answer different questions.</div>"
$LEARN[5] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Assigning an issue to Copilot starts a full lifecycle. First the agent <b>acknowledges</b>: GitHub shows an eyes reaction on the issue meaning 'accepted, I am on it'. Then it opens a branch, commits work in progress and pushes, and the <b>PR timeline</b> narrates each phase with status updates. Finally it opens a draft PR and CI runs. Many repos require a human to <b>Approve and run</b> workflows on agent PRs (a fork-safety habit), so checks can sit idle waiting for you: that is normal, not broken. <b>Example timeline reading:</b> eyes reaction, no branch after 10 minutes means the agent never started (check assignment and permissions). Branch plus commits plus a 55-minute-old status means it stalled mid-run (nudge it). Draft PR with grey idle checks means go click Approve and run. Each signal maps to exactly one next action.</div>"
$LEARN[6] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI can run <b>hooks</b>: shell scripts stored under .github/hooks that fire on lifecycle events. Think of them as tripwires. <b>preToolUse</b> fires before a tool runs and can veto it. <b>postToolUse</b> fires after a tool completes, good for logging or follow-up checks. <b>userPromptSubmitted</b> fires when you send a prompt. <b>sessionStart / sessionEnd</b> fire once per session. <b>errorOccurred</b> fires when an agent execution fails. <b>Example:</b> a team pages on-call whenever nightly agent runs fail: an errorOccurred hook running a curl to the paging API fires only on failure. A postToolUse hook would page on every tool call, and sessionEnd would page even on success. Matching the hook to the event is the whole skill: before versus after, every-call versus on-failure.</div>"
$LEARN[7] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI has working styles with opposite philosophies. Normal/agent mode acts immediately. <b>Plan mode</b> investigates first: it reads the repo, researches, then presents a written approach and waits. Nothing changes until you approve, at which point it implements the agreed plan. Flags modify autonomy: <b>--allow-all</b> pre-approves every tool (maximum speed, minimum safety), and slash commands do chores: <b>/compact</b> shrinks context, <b>/usage</b> shows tokens. <b>Example:</b> asked to refactor authentication, plan mode returns 'I will extract a handler, update 4 controllers, add tests' and pauses. You spot it missed the mobile client, correct the plan, approve, and only then does code change. Starting with --agent=Task merely picks a persona, and --allow-all skips the very review the requirement demands.</div>"
$LEARN[8] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The coding agent plans from the issue text, so issue quality caps result quality. A one-liner like 'fix login' hides scope (which login: web, mobile, SSO?), expected behavior, reproduction steps and acceptance criteria, and the agent fills the gaps by guessing. A good issue states problem, context, desired outcome and how to verify. Nothing else in the option list feeds the agent better input: memory tunes style, MCP limits tune doc retrieval, runner resources tune speed. <b>Example:</b> 'Users report login fails' produces a PR touching the wrong login form. 'On the web React form, SSO login loops after password reset; reproduce with a federated test account; done means the loop is gone and existing tests pass' produces a scoped, verifiable PR. When reviews complain an agent PR 'missed the point', rewrite the issue first.</div>"
$LEARN[9] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> An agent profile (the YAML frontmatter of a .agent.md file) has two halves that beginners confuse. The <b>tools</b> key is a machine-enforced allow-list: only listed tools can run. The <b>instructions</b> prose is guidance the model reads: influential but not enforced. 'Use all available tools' written in prose grants nothing. Separately, <b>MCP servers</b> (external tool providers like MCP1) are wired through repository MCP configuration files, not through the agent profile. <b>Example:</b> agent1 shows tools: [read,search,edit]. The planned change 'access all tools' is achieved by deleting that line so the default (everything) applies. MCP1 access comes from the repo mcp.json change, a different planned change. Adding an mcp-servers block to the profile answers the wrong change, and tools: [] would lock the agent out of everything.</div>"
$LEARN[10] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Secrets have homes, and each home serves exactly one runtime. <b>Actions secrets</b> inject into workflow runs via secrets context. <b>Codespaces secrets</b> inject into your cloud dev environment. The <b>Copilot environment</b> (with the COPILOT_MCP_ prefix convention) injects into the coding agent and its MCP servers, referenced by variable name from mcp.json. Anything committed to git, a plaintext apiKey field, an instructions file, an agent config, is readable by everyone with repo access: that is a leak, not storage. <b>Example mcp.json:</b> the server entry sets apiKey to the COPILOT_MCP_MCP1 variable instead of the literal key. The literal key works today and leaks forever; the variable resolves only inside the agent runtime. When a question asks where an agent credential lives, first ask which runtime consumes it, then pick that runtime home.</div>"
$LEARN[11] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> An MCP server exposes up to three surfaces and they are independent. <b>Tools</b> are callable functions: do this for me. <b>Resources</b> are readable data: let me see that. <b>Prompts</b> are reusable templates: fill this in. A client enables each surface separately, and the GitHub coding agent consumes configured tools. Remote servers re-advertise their catalog every session, so tools added server-side appear client-side with no config edit. <b>Example:</b> MCP1 offers create_ticket and deploy_build tools: agent1 calls them autonomously once configured. If Litware adds close_ticket next week, it just appears. But ticket attachments (resources) and ticket templates (prompts) were never configured as usable surfaces, so statements claiming the agent can use them are false even while tool statements are true. Always check which surface the question configured.</div>"
$LEARN[12] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> VS Code discovers MCP servers from <b>mcp.json</b> files in a fixed precedence: user profile settings (only you), then workspace <b>.vscode/mcp.json</b> (everyone opening this repo). Only the workspace file can be committed to git, which makes it the central lever: one file, whole team, all agents and developers, versioned and reviewable like code. Agent profile files (.agent.md) describe personas, and .vscode/settings.json holds local editor preferences: neither transports MCP servers. The &lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json style path scopes a single project coding-agent setup, not repo-wide Chat. <b>Example:</b> committing .vscode/mcp.json with the Learn server entry means a new hire opens repo1 and immediately validates designs against Microsoft Learn, with zero personal setup. That is what centrally means.</div>"
$LEARN[13] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Language models read only a fixed-size <b>context window</b>: everything older gets pushed out, and the agent starts forgetting your goals, then hallucinating. Long agentic sessions (reproducing failures, iterating on approaches) fill the window fast. The CLI slash commands each do one chore: <b>/compact</b> summarizes the conversation so far into a compact brief and continues from it, <b>/context</b> shows what is currently loaded, <b>/clear</b> throws history away, <b>/yolo</b> toggles auto-approval. <b>Example:</b> after two hours of debugging, the CLI warns the context is nearly full. /compact distills 200 tool calls into 'tried X, failed at Y, next: Z' and work continues with full memory of conclusions. /clear would also free space, but it amputates the conclusions too. Compact preserves, clear discards: that is the whole difference.</div>"
$LEARN[14] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Protecting sensitive paths needs two cooperating mechanisms. A <b>branch protection rule (or ruleset)</b> is the lock: it can require pull-request reviews, status checks and named reviewers before merge on matching paths such as .github/workflows/* and /infra/*. A <b>CODEOWNERS</b> file is the keyholder list: lines mapping paths to teams or users who must review. Either alone is half a solution: a review requirement with no designated reviewer can be satisfied by anyone; designated owners with no enforcement can be bypassed. <b>Example CODEOWNERS:</b> a line assigning /infra/* to the security team plus a rule requiring CODEOWNERS review means every infra change waits for security approval, and admins see exactly who is responsible. agents.md is not a GitHub file, .copilotignore only hides files from Copilot suggestions, and copilot-instructions.md guides the agent but cannot force a human to approve.</div>"
$LEARN[15] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Unattended automation combines three CLI switches with three jobs. <b>--autopilot</b> tells the agent to keep working without asking. <b>--yolo</b> pre-approves every tool so nothing pauses for confirmation. <b>--max-autopilot-continues N</b> caps how many autonomous rounds may run: the emergency brake. The script also uses set -euo pipefail so the shell itself fails fast on errors. <b>Example:</b> 'update dependencies, run tests, open a PR' with --yolo lets the agent run package managers and git without prompts. If a dependency conflict makes it retry forever, the continue-cap stops it at 10 rounds instead of burning budget all night. Prevention of runaway loops is Yes, all-tools access is Yes, and precisely because nothing asks, targeted human intervention is No: autonomy and oversight are traded against each other, and this command spends all of it on autonomy.</div>"
